'''Vitruvian''' (vi-trö′vi-an), ''a.'' [< L. ''Vitruvius'' (see def.) + ''-an.''] Of or pertaining to Marcus Vitruvius Pollio, a Roman architect of the latter part of the first century {{sc|b. c.}}, the author of an important treatise on architecture, which, although its statements can be accepted only after careful criticism, preserves much that is valuable regarding Greek and Roman art.—{{smaller|'''Vitruvian scroll''', an architectural ornament named after Vitruvius, consisting of a series of convoluted scrolls, of}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Vitruvian Scroll.—From Palazzo Pesaro, Venice.}}}}
{{smaller|fanciful and varied effect. It frequently occurs in friezes}}

